        "original_id": "fac8f2c2-9af5-5d8b-9de0-c563a64fd808",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jefferson Davis, Alexander H. Stephens, Daniel S. Lamont, Franklin Pierce, Grover Cleveland\n- Roles: President of the Confederate States, Vice President of the Confederate States, United States Secretary of War\n- Places: White House of the Confederacy\n- Organizations: Confederate States of America, United States\n- Events: Confederate States presidential election of 1861\n\nFacts:\n- The White House of the Confederacy was built in 1818.\n- Jefferson Davis served as the 23rd United States Secretary of War under U.S. President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\n- Daniel Scott Lamont was born on February 9, 1851.\n- In the Confederate States presidential election of 1861, Jefferson Davis and Alexander H. Stephens, who had held office under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America for six-year terms running from February 22, 1862, to February 22, 1868.\n- The White House of the Confederacy was the main executive residence of Jefferson Davis from August 1861 until April 1865.\n- Daniel S. Lamont served as United States Secretary of War during Grover Cleveland's second term.\n- Daniel Scott Lamont died on July 23, 1905.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "55b4dc54-579a-56d8-af56-7eac93a3b4fb",
        "text": "Page: Confederate States presidential election, 1861. Sentence: Jefferson Davis, who had been elected President and Alexander H. Stephens, who had been elected Vice President, under the Provisional Confederate States Constitution, were elected to six-year terms (February 22, 1862 – February 22, 1868) as the first permanent President and Vice President of the Confederate States of America.\nPage: Jefferson Davis. Sentence: He was the 23rd United States Secretary of War, serving under U.S. President Franklin Pierce from 1853 to 1857.\nPage: White House of the Confederacy. Sentence: Built in 1818, it was the main executive residence of the sole President of the Confederate States of America, Jefferson Davis, from August 1861 until April 1865.\nPage: Daniel S. Lamont. Sentence: Daniel Scott Lamont (February 9, 1851 – July 23, 1905) was the United States Secretary of War during Grover Cleveland's second term.",
